WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:05.820
Margot Robbie certainly didn't suffer the&nbsp;
agony of "defeat" to pull off this shot.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:10.200
But how did it come together in the&nbsp;
first place? Movie trickery is afoot.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:14.940
Are you wondering exactly how Margot Robbie pulled&nbsp;
off that shot in the "Barbie" trailer where she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:20.280
steps out of her heel onto a perfectly arched&nbsp;
foot? Apparently, a lot of people were — so Robbie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:25.140
ended up having to address the whole shebang while&nbsp;
promoting her upcoming film. How many takes was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:31.740
it? Was the shot created using movie magic? Did&nbsp;
Robbie actually do it? Not that many, no, and yes.

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:35.280
As Robbie revealed in a behind-the-scenes&nbsp;
interview with Fandango,

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:43.140
"It was probably about eight takes. Wasn't that&nbsp;
many. They are my feet. I walked up, we had little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:47.940
sticky bits on the floor for the double sided&nbsp;
tape for the shoes, so they wouldn't come off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:52.080
so that I could get my feet out of them. And I was&nbsp;
holding onto a bar, but that's it. I wasn't in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:55.860
harness or anything like that. I just walked&nbsp;
up, kind of held onto the bar above camera."

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:59.640
Asked if she was a dancer based&nbsp;
on her high arch, Robbie said,

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:01.680
"I did ballet when I was a kid."

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:05.760
But still, her point was that she&nbsp;
wanted to do the shot herself:

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:10.200
"I always try and do my own inserts.&nbsp;
I don't like when I watch a movie and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:13.800
I know it's not my hands. I hate that&nbsp;
so much. I always say to the director,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:17.880
'please let me do all my own things.' I&nbsp;
don't like knowing that I didn't do it."

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:21.060
If you're anyone who ever&nbsp;
played with Barbies as a kid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:25.860
you know exactly why this shot was so vital&nbsp;
to the trailer and the overall film. Those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:30.420
who have played with a Barbie know that her&nbsp;
plastic foot is always perfectly arched,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:34.560
ready to slip into whichever pair of&nbsp;
shoes you decide to choose next — and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:39.060
the journey Robbie's Barbie takes in the movie&nbsp;
is revealed through her lack of a sharp arch.

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:42.360
When Barbie's foot goes flat,&nbsp;
she shows her Barbie friends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:47.100
only to receive a horrified reaction as&nbsp;
they realize she's less than perfect. Other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:51.360
strange happenings are going on too — her&nbsp;
clothes don't dress her on their own anymore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:55.920
she can't float off her roof without falling&nbsp;
and injuring herself, and she can't stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.920 --> 00:02:01.260
thinking about dying. Perplexed, Barbie visits&nbsp;
Weird Barbie, a Barbie erratically styled by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:06.180
her owners and played by Kate McKinnon,&nbsp;
who presents... a shoe-related conundrum.

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:08.820
"You have to go to the real world."

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:13.680
You might look at Margot Robbie and think,&nbsp;
"yeah, that's what Barbie looks like," and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:16.680
you probably wouldn't be alone —&nbsp;
in a Time feature about the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:21.780
Mattel executives basically stop short of&nbsp;
saying exactly that. Robbie, for her part,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:25.440
was smart enough to know that this movie&nbsp;
needs to be more than a close look at how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:30.360
Barbie is blonde and beautiful — and along with&nbsp;
writer-director Greta Gerwig, she made sure that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:34.680
the film features a diverse array of actors&nbsp;
playing Barbies. Robbie told the magazine,

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:39.000
"If [Mattel] hadn't made that change to have a&nbsp;
multiplicity of Barbies, I don't think I would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:43.140
have wanted to attempt to make a Barbie film.&nbsp;
I don't think you should say, 'This is the one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:48.360
version of what Barbie is, and that's what women&nbsp;
should aspire to be and look like and act like.'"

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:52.440
"Insecure" star and creator Issa Rae,&nbsp;
who plays President Barbie in the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:55.020
clearly agrees; in the same feature, she said

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:59.040
"My worry was that it was going to feel&nbsp;
too white feminist-y, but I think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:04.080
it's self-aware. Barbie Land is perfect, right?&nbsp;
It represents perfection. So if perfection is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:07.560
just a bunch of white Barbies, I don't know&nbsp;
that anybody can get on board with that."

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:13.560
Not only did Robbie and Gerwig craft an inclusive&nbsp;
and diverse world full of Barbies all crushing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:18.360
it in their own right, but, by their own account,&nbsp;
they worked together to create a fun, subversive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:22.980
and clever film even though it's centered&nbsp;
around a toy and overseen by a toy company.

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:27.480
They still ran into some problems when it came&nbsp;
to the Mattel overlords, though. According&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:33.120
to the Time article, Robbie and Gerwig had to&nbsp;
perform one scene live for President and COO of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:38.100
Mattel Richard Dickson, who objected to... well,&nbsp;
whatever happens in it, because we still don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:42.840
know. Performing it in person worked, though, and&nbsp;
he agreed to keep the scene. Robbie clarified,

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:46.980
"When you look on the page, the nuance&nbsp;
isn't there, the delivery isn't there."

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:50.220
Ultimately, it's about being in&nbsp;
on the joke... and apparently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:54.480
that's a concept Robbie had to&nbsp;
explain to Mattel's CEO Ynon Kreiz.

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:59.640
"In that very first meeting, we impressed upon&nbsp;
Ynon we are going to honor the legacy of your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:03.180
brand, but if we don't acknowledge&nbsp;
certain things—if we don't say it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:06.780
someone else is going to say it. So you might&nbsp;
as well be a part of that conversation."

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:10.680
Robbie definitely took charge of the&nbsp;
"Barbie" movie — and as a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:14.940
it looks amazing, including that&nbsp;
iconic foot shot performed by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:19.500
Oscar nominee herself. "Barbie"&nbsp;
hits theaters on July 21, 2023.

